WEBVTT

NOTE Say what the odds mean

1
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:10.845
In February 1961, the Joint Chiefs of Staff reviewed a United States plan to invade

2
00:00:10.845 --> 00:00:12.767
Cuba at the Bay of Pigs.

3
00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:16.004
Their report listed serious problems.

4
00:00:16.004 --> 00:00:21.700
Yet its final judgment said the plan "has a fair chance of ultimate success."

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.782
The officer who wrote that sentence later explained the intended odds: about three

6
00:00:27.782 --> 00:00:30.900
chances in ten. He meant "fair" as a warning.

7
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.874
Other readers heard support for the plan.

8
00:00:33.874 --> 00:00:38.958
The Central Intelligence Agency quoted the phrase while arguing that the invasion

9
00:00:38.958 --> 00:00:39.900
should proceed.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.106
President John F. Kennedy believed the military leaders had supported the plan.

11
00:00:45.106 --> 00:00:49.000
After the invasion failed, he asked why they had not warned him.

12
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:54.542
Three chances in ten of success also meant seven chances in ten of failure.

13
00:00:54.542 --> 00:00:58.980
Where did those seven chances disappear between writer and reader?

14
00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.400
How can we keep that from happening?

15
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:08.579
Communication succeeds only when the listener understands the odds the speaker

16
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:14.467
meant. Words, numbers, groups, and pictures can each change the message.

17
00:01:14.967 --> 00:01:21.647
We will follow six points where meaning can change: the word, the number's group,

18
00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:27.833
the picture, one result, words tied to actions, and the complete statement.

19
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:29.822
Begin with the word.

20
00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:35.378
Ten years before the Bay of Pigs, Sherman Kent found the same problem in another

21
00:01:35.378 --> 00:01:36.767
intelligence report.

22
00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:43.594
In March 1951, an official forecast said a Soviet attack on Yugoslavia "should be

23
00:01:43.594 --> 00:01:46.333
considered a serious possibility."

24
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:48.852
Kent helped approve that phrase.

25
00:01:48.852 --> 00:01:52.825
He thought it meant about sixty-five chances in one hundred.

26
00:01:52.825 --> 00:01:56.467
A State Department planner read it as much less likely.

27
00:01:56.867 --> 00:02:01.811
Other board members gave meanings from twenty to eighty chances in one hundred.

28
00:02:01.811 --> 00:02:06.567
Everyone had approved the same words, but they had not shared the same odds.

29
00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:13.040
Kent proposed linking each word to a number range. Agencies now use such scales.

30
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:18.415
United States intelligence uses likely for fifty-five to eighty percent.

31
00:02:18.415 --> 00:02:24.239
The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change uses likely for sixty-six to one

32
00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:25.433
hundred percent.

33
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:30.783
A sixty-percent chance is therefore likely on the intelligence scale.

34
00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:35.733
On the climate scale, the same number is only about as likely as not.

35
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:40.116
Readers also pull strong words toward the middle.

36
00:02:40.116 --> 00:02:46.296
In a 2012 study, people read very likely, meant as at least ninety percent, as

37
00:02:46.296 --> 00:02:51.367
sixty-two percent. They read very unlikely as forty-one percent.

38
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:57.442
A 2019 study tested a simple fix: put the number in brackets beside the word.

39
00:02:57.442 --> 00:03:02.800
Readers matched the official range about twice as well when they saw both.

40
00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:08.500
A word does not carry fixed odds by itself. Put the number beside it.

41
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:14.567
The number makes the word clearer. Next ask what that number counts.

42
00:03:14.867 --> 00:03:18.440
Suppose tomorrow has a thirty-percent chance of rain.

43
00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:24.103
In a 2005 study, respondents often thought that meant rain for thirty percent of the

44
00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.867
day or across thirty percent of the area.

45
00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:30.193
The weather forecast means something else.

46
00:03:30.193 --> 00:03:35.906
Rain occurs on about three out of ten days with conditions like tomorrow's at that

47
00:03:35.906 --> 00:03:36.533
location.

48
00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:40.300
About two out of three New Yorkers chose that meaning.

49
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.100
In four European cities, only one out of five to one out of three people did.

50
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:51.457
The missing piece is the reference class, meaning the group of cases being counted.

51
00:03:51.457 --> 00:03:55.333
Name that group. Otherwise, listeners will invent one.

52
00:03:56.333 --> 00:04:00.254
Clear words and numbers still need a clear picture.

53
00:04:00.254 --> 00:04:04.867
A picture can hide uncertainty even while trying to show it.

54
00:04:05.167 --> 00:04:07.208
Consider a hurricane forecast.

55
00:04:07.208 --> 00:04:12.585
The National Hurricane Center draws a cone around the predicted track to show a

56
00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:14.967
likely area for the storm's center.

57
00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:18.514
The cone is based on past forecast errors.

58
00:04:18.514 --> 00:04:24.809
About two thirds of storm centers stay inside it, which means about one third travel

59
00:04:24.809 --> 00:04:25.633
outside it.

60
00:04:26.033 --> 00:04:31.005
In August 2004, Hurricane Charley's forecast line ran near Tampa.

61
00:04:31.005 --> 00:04:35.900
The storm struck Punta Gorda instead, about seventy miles south.

62
00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:41.525
Punta Gorda and the surrounding county had remained inside the cone for four days.

63
00:04:41.525 --> 00:04:45.667
Much television coverage still drew attention to the center line.

64
00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:51.650
National Hurricane Center director Max Mayfield later joked that his tombstone

65
00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:55.300
should say, "Don't focus on the skinny black line."

66
00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.762
Viewers may treat a forecast line as a promised path.

67
00:04:59.762 --> 00:05:05.667
Every uncertainty picture must explain what it includes and what it leaves out.

68
00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:11.642
Now suppose the word, number, group, and picture are clear.

69
00:05:11.642 --> 00:05:16.533
One more trap remains: judging the forecast by one result.

70
00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:22.192
In its final 2016 forecast, FiveThirtyEight gave Donald Trump a twenty-nine-percent

71
00:05:22.192 --> 00:05:25.033
chance of winning the presidential election.

72
00:05:25.433 --> 00:05:30.767
Other models gave him fifteen percent. Some placed his chance below one percent.

73
00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:36.130
Trump won. That result did not prove the twenty-nine-percent forecast wrong.

74
00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:41.616
Events with a twenty-nine-percent chance should occur about twenty-nine times out of

75
00:05:41.616 --> 00:05:42.400
one hundred.

76
00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:50.067
One result cannot test a probability. Explain that before anyone knows the result.

77
00:05:51.067 --> 00:05:56.999
If words can confuse people, should every field replace them with numbers? No.

78
00:05:56.999 --> 00:05:59.433
Some words set rules for action.

79
00:05:59.733 --> 00:06:02.233
Accounting provides one example.

80
00:06:02.233 --> 00:06:08.794
A rule called Accounting Standards Codification 450 sorts possible losses into three

81
00:06:08.794 --> 00:06:12.700
groups: probable, reasonably possible, and remote.

82
00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:18.505
The rule defines these terms with words, not fixed percentages.

83
00:06:18.505 --> 00:06:24.167
Probable means likely to occur. Remote means the chance is slight.

84
00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.426
Each term leads to an accounting action.

85
00:06:27.426 --> 00:06:31.572
A probable loss that can be estimated enters the accounts.

86
00:06:31.572 --> 00:06:36.933
A reasonably possible loss gets a note. A remote loss usually gets neither.

87
00:06:37.333 --> 00:06:39.840
Courts also use word-based levels.

88
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:45.370
In the 1979 case Addington against Texas, the Supreme Court discussed three

89
00:06:45.370 --> 00:06:50.900
standards of proof. A standard tells a judge or jury how sure they must be.

90
00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:56.154
In most civil disputes, the two sides bear the risk of error more evenly.

91
00:06:56.154 --> 00:07:00.343
In criminal cases, the government bears much more of that risk.

92
00:07:00.343 --> 00:07:04.067
Clear and convincing evidence lies between those levels.

93
00:07:04.467 --> 00:07:08.354
The Court did not attach percentages to these standards.

94
00:07:08.354 --> 00:07:12.867
It said their exact effect on decisions "may well be unknowable."

95
00:07:13.367 --> 00:07:18.739
Some word scales exist to guide action, not to report exact odds.

96
00:07:18.739 --> 00:07:23.533
Before adding a number, ask what the scale is meant to do.

97
00:07:24.533 --> 00:07:31.477
We have checked the word, number, group, picture, result, and kind of scale.

98
00:07:31.477 --> 00:07:34.400
One final part is still missing.

99
00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:40.978
In 2019, van der Bles and other researchers described the parts of a useful

100
00:07:40.978 --> 00:07:44.912
uncertainty statement. State what is uncertain.

101
00:07:44.912 --> 00:07:49.767
Then show its form, such as a probability, range, or word.

102
00:07:50.167 --> 00:07:54.299
The statement should also describe the strength of the evidence.

103
00:07:54.299 --> 00:07:56.882
The climate panel calls this confidence.

104
00:07:56.882 --> 00:08:01.467
It depends on how much evidence exists and how well the findings agree.

105
00:08:01.867 --> 00:08:06.089
Confidence and probability answer different questions.

106
00:08:06.089 --> 00:08:09.139
Probability gives the odds of an event.

107
00:08:09.139 --> 00:08:14.300
Confidence tells us how strongly the evidence supports those odds.

108
00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.636
Here is a complete example.

109
00:08:16.636 --> 00:08:22.158
Rain tomorrow is likely, with a chance between fifty-five and eighty percent.

110
00:08:22.158 --> 00:08:26.962
That range refers to days with similar conditions at this location.

111
00:08:26.962 --> 00:08:29.400
Confidence in the range is medium.

112
00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:35.525
Give the word, the number, the group being counted, and the strength of the

113
00:08:35.525 --> 00:08:36.200
evidence.

114
00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.334
The full message now comes together. Put the number beside the word.

115
00:08:42.334 --> 00:08:48.600
Name the group behind the number. Explain what the picture includes and leaves out.

116
00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:52.755
Warn that one result cannot test the forecast.

117
00:08:52.755 --> 00:08:56.107
Keep word scales when they guide action.

118
00:08:56.107 --> 00:09:00.800
Then state how strongly the evidence supports the claim.

119
00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:04.510
Where did seven chances in ten disappear?

120
00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.848
They vanished inside the phrase fair chance.

121
00:09:07.848 --> 00:09:13.917
The report gave no number, no comparison group, and no account of the evidence's

122
00:09:13.917 --> 00:09:14.600
strength.

123
00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:18.002
A clearer sentence might have said this.

124
00:09:18.002 --> 00:09:23.299
The plan has about three chances in ten of success, compared with similar

125
00:09:23.299 --> 00:09:27.000
operations. Our confidence in that estimate is low.

126
00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:34.412
Fifteen years later, the officer who wrote "a fair chance" still regretted that he

127
00:09:34.412 --> 00:09:41.390
had not insisted on numbers. Give the word, number, group, and strength of evidence.

128
00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.633
Make the odds hard to miss.
